Solution 1. Clearly, \(\angle AMB = 150^\circ\) and \(\angle AMC = 110^\circ\). Now by the Law of Sines on triangles \(ABM\) and \(ACM\), we have \(\frac{AB}{\sin 150^\circ} = \frac{AM}{\sin 20^\circ}\) and \(\frac{AC}{\sin 110^\circ} = \frac{AM}{\sin 30^\circ}.\) Combining these equations gives us \(\frac{AB}{AC} = \frac{\sin 150^\circ \sin 30^\circ}{\sin 20^\circ \sin 110^\circ}.\) Without loss of generality, let \(AB = \sin 150^\circ \sin 30^\circ = \frac{1}{4}\) and \(AC = \sin 20^\circ \sin 110^\circ\). Then by the Law of Cosines, we have
\begin{align*} BC^2 &= AB^2 + AC^2 - 2(AB)(BC)\cos\angle BAC\\ &= \frac{1}{16} + \sin^2 20^\circ\sin^2 110^\circ - 2\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)\sin 20^\circ\sin 110^\circ\cos 50^\circ \\ &= \frac{1}{16} + \sin^2 20^\circ \sin^2 110^\circ - \frac{1}{2}\sin 20^\circ\sin 110^\circ\sin 40^\circ \\ &= \frac{1}{16} + \sin^2 20^\circ \sin^2 110^\circ - \sin 20^\circ\sin 110^\circ\sin 20^\circ\cos 20^\circ \\ &= \frac{1}{16} \end{align*}
Thus, \(AB = BC\), our desired conclusion.
